From: thingino sigmastar port
Subject: [PATCH] hw_random: sstar-rng: credit conservatively, reject a stuck RNG

The SoC has a hardware TRNG in the RSA/crypto block, reachable through
HAL_RNG_Read(). The vendor already wires it up: mdrv_aes.c calls
sstar_rng_probe() from the AES driver's probe under CONFIG_SS_RNG, which is why
the platform_driver block at the bottom of this file is commented out -- it is
dead by design, not by accident. Only the Kconfig symbol was missing.

Enabling it fixes a real boot stall. This board has almost no entropy at boot:
no ethernet, no disk, and wlan0 does not exist yet, while S02ssl generates the
uhttpd certificate through mbedTLS, whose entropy poll uses a blocking
getrandom(). Init waits at S02 for a CRNG only later stages of the same init
sequence could seed. With a serial console attached, typing gets past it;
headless, it just looks hung.

Two changes, both about not overstating what this hardware gives us.

1. Quality 500 -> 128. The hwrng core credits rc * quality * 8 >> 10 bits per
   read, so 500 claims just under 4 bits of entropy per byte. 128 credits
   exactly 1 bit per byte: an 8x margin against full credit, and ~4x more
   conservative than the vendor's figure. Nobody here has run a statistical
   suite against this TRNG and the vendor's number is unsourced, so this is
   chosen to be defensible rather than accurate. It is tunable at runtime via
   /sys/module/hw_random/parameters/current_quality, which hwrng_fillfn
   re-reads every iteration.

2. Reject a stuck generator. HAL_RNG_Read() polls a ready bit 20 times and then
   reads the data register anyway, so a dead or unclocked RNG returns stale or
   constant values rather than an error -- and crediting entropy for those is
   worse than crediting none, because it fails silently. Comparing against the
   previous value is the NIST SP 800-90B repetition count test in its cheapest
   form. Retrying first avoids a spurious 10-second back-off on the ~1-in-65536
   chance repeat a healthy 16-bit source produces.

Applies to Infinity6E and Infinity6B0 alike; the driver is byte-identical in
both OpenIPC kernel trees and RSA_BASE_ADDR is 0x112200 on both.

--- a/drivers/sstar/crypto/char/hw_random/sstar-rng.c	2026-07-31 18:05:45.471754776 -0700
+++ b/drivers/sstar/crypto/char/hw_random/sstar-rng.c	2026-07-31 18:05:45.497046814 -0700
@@ -32,13 +32,49 @@
     struct hwrng rng;
 };
 
+/*
+ * Attempts to get a value differing from the previous one before declaring the
+ * generator stuck. A healthy 16-bit source repeats about 1 read in 65536 by
+ * chance; five consecutive identical values put that at 2^-64.
+ */
+#define SSTAR_RNG_STUCK_RETRIES 5
+
 static int sstar_rng_read(struct hwrng *rng, void *buf, size_t max, bool wait)
 {
     //struct sstar_rng *hrng = to_sstar_rng(rng);
     u16 *data = buf;
-    //printk("[%s,%d] max(%d)\n",__FUNCTION__,__LINE__, max);
-    *data = HAL_RNG_Read();
-    return 2;
+    static u16 prev;
+    static bool have_prev;
+    u16 val = 0;
+    int tries;
+
+    if (max < sizeof(*data))
+        return 0;
+
+    /*
+     * HAL_RNG_Read() polls a ready bit 20 times and then reads the data
+     * register anyway, so a dead or unclocked RNG returns stale or constant
+     * values rather than an error. Feeding those to the pool while crediting
+     * entropy for them is worse than crediting nothing: the pool fills, boot
+     * proceeds, and every key is drawn from a stuck source.
+     */
+    for (tries = 0; tries < SSTAR_RNG_STUCK_RETRIES; tries++) {
+        val = HAL_RNG_Read();
+        if (!have_prev || val != prev)
+            break;
+    }
+
+    if (have_prev && val == prev) {
+        pr_warn_ratelimited("sstar-rng: %d identical reads (0x%04x), generator appears stuck\n",
+                            SSTAR_RNG_STUCK_RETRIES, val);
+        return 0;
+    }
+
+    prev = val;
+    have_prev = true;
+
+    *data = val;
+    return sizeof(*data);
 }
 
 int sstar_rng_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
@@ -63,7 +99,14 @@
     rng->rng.init = NULL;;
     rng->rng.cleanup = NULL;
     rng->rng.read = sstar_rng_read;
-    rng->rng.quality = 500;
+    /*
+     * 1 bit of credited entropy per byte read; the hwrng core credits
+     * rc * quality * 8 >> 10. Conservative on purpose -- the vendor's 500
+     * (just under 4 bits/byte) is unsourced and this TRNG has not been
+     * characterised here. Tunable live, without a rebuild, via
+     * /sys/module/hw_random/parameters/current_quality.
+     */
+    rng->rng.quality = 128;
 
     ret = devm_hwrng_register(&pdev->dev, &rng->rng);
     if (ret) {
